   "id": "fix-opening-cliff",
   "category": "content",
   "text": "The opening loses almost everyone: E1 goes from 67.4 of every 100 watching near the start to 11.8 at the 2% mark, and E6 falls from 74.5 to 15.9. Every episode shows the same early cliff.",
   "recommendation": "Script a tight one-minute cold open this week that states the episode's payoff up front, then jump straight to the first demo. Push the warm-up chatter until after the hook and check whether the 2% mark hold improves next episode."
  },
  {
   "id": "replicate-e1-subscribe-ask",
   "category": "audience",
   "text": "Among finished episodes, E1 converted 8.9 subscribers per 1,000 views while E2 got 2.9 and E3 got 0. On DesignerTom, E1 hit 13.3 per 1,000 vs 5.7 on Dive Club — the only episode where DesignerTom converted better.",
   "recommendation": "Rewatch E1 this week and copy whatever it did differently — the on-air subscribe ask, pinned comment, or title framing — into the next episode as a test, then check subscribers per 1,000 views once the episode has matured."
  },
  {
   "id": "lead-with-dive-club",
   "category": "distribution",
   "text": "Dive Club beats DesignerTom across the whole show: 5,144 views vs 3,707, 17 subscribers gained vs 8, 3.3 vs 2.2 subscribers per 1,000 views, and 11.3 vs 9.6 average share watched.",
   "recommendation": "Make Dive Club the primary home this week: premiere the full episode there with a title and thumbnail tailored to that audience, and test whether DesignerTom performs better with a shorter edited cut instead of the full stream."
  },
  {
   "id": "convert-x-viewers",
   "category": "promotion",
   "text": "X carries more watching than YouTube — 12,791 broadcast plays vs 9,875 YouTube views, with 56.4 of every 100 views happening on X — yet all subscriber growth lives on YouTube.",
   "recommendation": "Pin the YouTube replay link in every X broadcast and say it out loud mid-show this week. Post a follow-up X thread the next morning pointing to the YouTube replay so X viewers have a clear path to become YouTube subscribers."
  },
  {
   "id": "unlock-discovery-surfaces",
   "category": "distribution",
   "text": "54.8 of every 100 YouTube views come from existing subscribers. Discovery surfaces bring almost nothing: search is 4.9, playlists 0.7, end screens 0.1, and Shorts delivered just 2 views across the whole show.",
   "recommendation": "Cut three Shorts this week from the strongest demo moments, add an end screen on every upload pointing to the newest episode, and build one public playlist of all episodes on both channels — then watch which surface moves first."
  },
  {
   "id": "front-load-demos",
   "category": "content",
   "text": "In finished episode E3, the one-shot game showcase about three quarters of the way in, around minute 87, gained 2.3 extra viewers of every 100 — while about 3.9 of every 100 left near minute 9 during the designer-of-the-week intro early on.",
   "recommendation": "Test re-ordering the next episode: put a screen-share showcase like the one-shot game segment within the first ten minutes and move intros later. These timings are approximate from the live recording, so judge the change by the middle-half hold afterward."
